        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "$\\frac{\\pi}{4}$",
          "isCorrect": true
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "$\\frac{\\pi}{2}$",
          "isCorrect": false
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "$\\frac{1}{2}$",
          "isCorrect": false
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "$1$",
          "isCorrect": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "Linéarisation : $\\cos^2 x = \\frac{1+\\cos(2x)}{2}$. Intégrale : $\\frac{1}{2}[x+\\frac{\\sin(2x)}{2}]_0^{\\pi/2} = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$."
    },
    {
      "id": "ensa-22-06",
      "type": "mcq",
      "theme": "analyse",
      "category": "Intégration",
      "difficulty": "concours",
      "prompt": "Soit $f$ continue sur $[0,1]$ avec $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 f(t)\\,dt = 3$. Alors $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 f(1-t)\\,dt$ vaut :",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "$3$",
          "isCorrect": true
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "$-3$",
          "isCorrect": false
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "$0$",
          "isCorrect": false
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "On ne peut pas conclure",
          "isCorrect": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "Changement de variable $u = 1-t$ : $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 f(1-t)dt = \\int_0^1 f(u)du = 3$. Propriété de symétrie de l'intégrale sur $[0,1]$."
    },
    {
